)\nConversion_Rate                      post-launch outcome (leakage)\nAcquisition_Cost              treated as an outcome (leakage risk)\nCTR                                derived from outcomes (leakage)\nCost_per_Click                     derived from outcomes (leakage)\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":154},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# WHY: a model must be judged on rows it has never seen. We also prepare a time-based split,\n# because in real life we train on the past and predict the future.\nX_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = train_test_split(X, y, test_size=0.20, random_state=RANDOM_STATE, shuffle=True)\nprint(\"Train:\", X_train.shape, \"| Test:\", X_test.shape)\nprint(f\"Train ROI mean={y_train.mean():.4f} std={y_train.std():.4f}\")\nprint(f\"Test  ROI mean={y_test.mean():.4f} std={y_test.std():.4f}\")   # should look almost identical\n\ncutoff = df[\"Date\"].quantile(0.80)                  # first 80% of dates = past, last 20% = future\ntime_train_idx = df.index[df[\"Date\"] <= cutoff]\ntime_test_idx  = df.index[df[\"Date\"] >  cutoff]\nprint(f\"\\nTime split cut-off date: {cutoff.date()} | train rows={len(time_train_idx)}, test rows={len(time_test_idx)}\")","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-03T08:11:13.437625Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-03T08:11:13.437964Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-03T08:11:13.49688Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-03T08:11:13.437938Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-03T08:11:13.496091Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Train: (160000, 9) | Test: (40000, 9)\nTrain ROI mean=5.0011 std=1.7354\nTest  ROI mean=5.0078 std=1.7307\n\nTime split cut-off date: 2021-10-19 | train rows=160016, test rows=39984\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":155},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# WHY: Ridge needs numbers only. One-hot encoding turns categories into 0/1 columns;\n# scaling puts Duration and Month on the same scale so no feature dominates.\npreprocessor = ColumnTransformer(transformers=[\n    (\"cat\", OneHotEncoder(handle_unknown=\"ignore\", drop=\"first\", sparse_output=False), FEATURES_CAT),\n    (\"num\", StandardScaler(), FEATURES_NUM),\n])\npreprocessor.fit(X_train)   # learn encodings from the TRAINING data only (no peeking at test data)\nn_out = preprocessor.transform(X_train).shape[1]\nprint(\"Columns going in :\", X_train.shape[1])\nprint(\"Columns coming out after encoding:\", n_out)\nprint(\"Example encoded names:\", list(preprocessor.get_feature_names_out()[:6]))","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-03T08:11:13.498164Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-03T08:11:13.498565Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-03T08:11:14.467811Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-03T08:11:13.498489Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-03T08:11:14.46697Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Columns going in : 9\nColumns coming out after encoding: 33\nExample encoded names: ['cat__Campaign_Type_Email', 'cat__Campaign_Type_Influencer', 'cat__Campaign_Type_Search', 'cat__Campaign_Type_Social Media', 'cat__Channel_Used_Facebook', 'cat__Channel_Used_Google Ads']\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":156},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# WHY: we train three things. Each answers a different question.\n#  1) Baseline  - \"always predict the average ROI\". Any real model must beat this.\n#  2) Ridge     - regularised linear model, our \"signal detector\" (it cannot memorise noise).\n#  3) GBM       - a flexible model used ONLY as a stress test: if even this finds nothing, nothing is there.\nbaseline = Pipeline([(\"prep\", preprocessor), (\"model\", DummyRegressor(strategy=\"mean\"))])\nridge = Pipeline([(\"prep\", preprocessor), (\"model\", Ridge(alpha=100.0, random_state=RANDOM_STATE))])\nstress = Pipeline([(\"prep\", preprocessor), (\"model\", HistGradientBoostingRegressor(\n    max_depth=3, learning_rate=0.05, max_iter=150, l2_regularization=1.0, random_state=RANDOM_STATE))])\nbaseline.fit(X_train, y_train); ridge.fit(X_train, y_train); stress.fit(X_train, y_train)\n\n# 4) THE MAIN COMPONENT: a benchmark estimator (not a predictor).\n#    For each group (e.g. Channel = Instagram) it stores the average ROI, a 95% confidence interval,\n#    and a \"shrunk\" average that pulls small groups toward the overall mean so they don't look\n#    falsely good or bad.\nclass BenchmarkEstimator:\n    def __init__(self, group_cols, shrink_k=200):\n        self.group_cols, self.shrink_k = group_cols, shrink_k\n    def fit(self, data, target=\"ROI\"):\n        self.target = target\n        self.global_mean = float(data[target].mean())\n        self.global_std = float(data[target].std())\n        self.table = {}\n        for col in self.group_cols:\n            g = data.groupby(col)[target].agg([\"count\", \"mean\", \"std\"])\n            g[\"shrunk_mean\"] = (g[\"count\"] * g[\"mean\"] + self.shrink_k * self.global_mean) / (g[\"count\"] + self.shrink_k)\n            g[\"ci95_half_width\"] = 1.96 * g[\"std\"] / np.sqrt(g[\"count\"])\n            self.table[col] = g\n        return self\n    def to_dict(self):\n        # Plain-dictionary export: safe to save as JSON and load in any backend (no class needed)\n        return {\"global_mean\": self.global_mean, \"global_std\": self.global_std,\n                \"shrink_k\": self.shrink_k, \"target\": self.target,\n                \"roi_percentile_points\": np.percentile(self.reference_roi, np.arange(101)).tolist(),\n                \"tables\": {c: t.reset_index().to_dict(orient=\"records\") for c, t in self.table.items()}}\n    def evaluate(self, group_col, group_value, observed_roi):\n        # Compare one campaign's ROI with its group's typical range\n        row = self.table[group_col].loc[group_value]\n        pct = float((self.reference_roi < observed_roi).mean() * 100)\n        lo, hi = row[\"mean\"] - row[\"ci95_half_width\"], row[\"mean\"] + row[\"ci95_half_width\"]\n        verdict = \"within typical range\" if lo <= observed_roi <= hi else (\n            \"above group average\" if observed_roi > hi else \"below group average\")\n        return {\"group\": f\"{group_col}={group_value}\", \"group_mean_roi\": round(row[\"mean\"], 3),\n                \"group_ci95\": (round(lo, 3), round(hi, 3)), \"n\": int(row[\"count\"]),\n                \"observed_roi\": observed_roi, \"percentile_overall\": round(pct, 1), \"verdict\": verdict}\n\nBENCH_GROUPS = [\"Campaign_Type\",\"Channel_Used\",\"Target_Audience\",\"Customer_Segment\",\"Location\",\"Language\",\"Day_of_Week\",\"Month\"]\nbenchmark = BenchmarkEstimator(BENCH_GROUPS).fit(df)\nbenchmark.reference_roi = df[\"ROI\"].values\nprint(\"Models trained: baseline, ridge, stress-test GBM, benchmark estimator\")\nprint(f\"Global mean ROI = {benchmark.global_mean:.3f}\")\nbenchmark.table[\"Channel_Used\"].round(3)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-03T08:11:14.468955Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-03T08:11:14.469321Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-03T08:11:16.571248Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-03T08:11:14.469297Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-03T08:11:16.570507Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Models trained: baseline, ridge, stress-test GBM, benchmark estimator\nGlobal mean ROI = 5.002\n","output_type":"stream"},{"execution_count":157,"output_type":"execute_result","data":{"text/plain":"              count   mean    std  shrunk_mean  ci95_half_width\nChannel_Used                                                   \nEmail         33599  4.996  1.738        4.997            0.019\nFacebook      32819  5.019  1.738        5.019            0.019\nGoogle Ads    33438  5.003  1.734        5.003            0.019\nInstagram     33392  4.989  1.739        4.989            0.019\nWebsite       33360  5.014  1.725        5.014            0.019\nYouTube       33392  4.994  1.732        4.994            0.019","text/html":"<div>\n<style scoped>\n    .dataframe tbody tr th:only-of-type {\n        vertical-align: